    "fullSolution": "**Evaluate $\\int \\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{9-x^2}}$**\n\n**Step 1: Substitution**\n$x = 3\\sin\\theta$, so $dx = 3\\cos\\theta\\, d\\theta$\n\n**Step 2: Simplify the radical**\n$$9 - x^2 = 9 - 9\\sin^2\\theta = 9(1-\\sin^2\\theta) = 9\\cos^2\\theta$$\n$$\\sqrt{9-x^2} = 3\\cos\\theta$$\n\n**Step 3: Transform integral**\n$$\\int \\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{9-x^2}} = \\int \\frac{3\\cos\\theta\\, d\\theta}{3\\cos\\theta} = \\int d\\theta = \\theta + C$$\n\n**Step 4: Back-substitute**\n$$x = 3\\sin\\theta \\implies \\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)$$\n\n**Answer:** $\\displaystyle \\arcsin\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) + C$",
